  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMid level water vapor satellite places an upper level low over \nOregon and Washington, poised to continue to move across the \nnorthern portion of CONUS. This low brought an end to the abnormally \nhot weather SoCal had experienced the past week, and allowed for the \nmarine layer to redevelop and thicken this morning. This system also \nhas aided in tightening the gradient, producing stronger onshore \nflow with afternoon gusts today and tomorrow around 35-50 mph \nthrough wind-prone passes and along the desert slopes. While this \nlow will meander eastward along the border with Canada on Monday,\na second trough will swing down from the PacNW and form a closed \nlow over southern Oregon and NorCal by Wednesday. This secondary \nlow will reinforce the cooler weather we've had, maintaining a \ncooling trend through at least Wednesday. As such, high \ntemperatures Mon-Wed will hover around 2-5 degrees below normal \nwith overnight low temperatures also slightly below normal for \neverywhere but the deserts which will remain a bit hotter with \nslightly better humidity to the east. The marine layer will \ncontinue to deepen and make it further inland each morning into at\nleast mid-week, with some hi-res guidance indicating it may be \ndeep enough to sustain some patchy morning drizzle on Tuesday, \nalthough chances of this occurring are low.\n\nThe low will weaken a bit as it becomes absorbed in the upper \nlevel pattern and pulled northward later this week. As the low \ndeparts, subsidence over the Pacific to the southwest of San Diego\nwill increase, with upper level ridging developing over CA Friday\nand Saturday. This will return the area to warming trend for the \nlate week, although guidance is chaotic with the long-range \npattern as the GFS attempts to return the region to troughing \nSunday and beyond while the ECMWF maintains the warmer ridge.",
